# Cursor Rules for Kalbook - Vercel Deployment Safety

## Project Context
- Next.js 14.2.6 with App Router
- TypeScript with strict mode enabled
- Supabase for database/auth (multi-tenant architecture)
- Edge runtime middleware
- Server/Client component architecture

## Critical Vercel Deployment Rules

### 1. Environment Variables
- ALWAYS check for required environment variables before using them
- Use non-null assertions (!) only when absolutely certain the variable exists
- Provide fallbacks or throw descriptive errors for missing env vars
- Required env vars:
  - `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL`
  - `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY`
  - `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` (server-side only)
  - `COOKIE_SECRET` (for cookie signing)
  - `BREVO_API_KEY` (for email)
  - `NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL` or `VERCEL_URL` (for absolute URLs)

Example pattern:
```typescript
const apiKey = process.env.BREVO_API_KEY;
if (!apiKey) {
  throw new Error('BREVO_API_KEY environment variable is required');
}
```

### 2. Edge Runtime Compatibility
- Middleware MUST use Edge-compatible APIs only
- NO Node.js APIs in middleware (fs, path, crypto, etc.)
- Use Web APIs: URL, Headers, Request, Response, fetch
- NO dynamic imports with Node.js modules in middleware
- Cookie operations must use Edge-compatible methods

Example:
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Edge compatible
export async function middleware(request: NextRequest) {
  const url = new URL(request.url);
  // ...
}

// ❌ BAD - Node.js API
import fs from 'fs'; // Will fail on Vercel Edge
```

### 3. Server vs Client Components
- Server Components (default): Can use async/await, access headers(), cookies(), etc.
- Client Components: Must have 'use client' directive, cannot use server-only APIs
- NEVER import server-only code in client components
- NEVER use 'use client' in API routes or middleware

Rules:
- API routes (`app/api/**/route.ts`) are always server-side - NO 'use client'
- Middleware is always Edge runtime - NO 'use client'
- Pages that use hooks, event handlers, or browser APIs need 'use client'
- Components that only render UI can be Server Components

### 4. TypeScript Build Safety
- ALWAYS fix TypeScript errors before deployment
- Use proper type assertions, avoid `any` when possible
- Ensure all imports resolve correctly
- Check that path aliases (@/*) are correctly configured
- Run `npm run build` locally to catch build errors

### 5. API Route Best Practices
- ALWAYS handle errors with try/catch
- ALWAYS return proper NextResponse.json() responses
- Include proper status codes (200, 400, 401, 404, 500)
- Validate request data before processing
- Use getTenantInfoFromRequest() for multi-tenant routes
- Never expose service role keys or secrets in responses

Example:
```typescript
export async function POST(request: NextRequest) {
  try {
    const tenantInfo = await getTenantInfoFromRequest(request);
    if (!tenantInfo?.businessId) {
      return NextResponse.json(
        { error: 'Business context required' },
        { status: 400 }
      );
    }
    // ... rest of logic
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('API error:', error);
    return NextResponse.json(
      { error: 'Internal server error' },
      { status: 500 }
    );
  }
}
```

### 6. File System Access
- NEVER use fs.readFile, fs.writeFile, or any Node.js file system APIs
- Use Supabase Storage for file uploads/downloads
- Use FormData for file handling in API routes
- Client-side file operations use File API (browser)

### 7. Dynamic Imports
- Use dynamic imports for code splitting: `const Component = dynamic(() => import('./Component'))`
- For conditional imports, ensure the module exists
- Avoid dynamic imports in middleware (Edge runtime limitations)

### 8. Supabase Client Usage
- Server Components: Use `createClient()` from `@/lib/supabase/server`
- Middleware: Use `createClient()` from `@/lib/supabase/middleware`
- Client Components: Use `supabase` from `@/lib/supabase/client`
- Admin operations: Use `createAdminClient()` from `@/lib/supabase/admin` (server-only)

### 9. Multi-Tenant Architecture
- ALWAYS get tenant context before database operations
- Use `getTenantInfoFromRequest()` in API routes
- Use `getTenantContext()` in Server Components
- Never query without business_id filter (RLS handles this, but be explicit)
- Validate business exists before operations

### 10. Error Handling
- Wrap async operations in try/catch
- Return user-friendly error messages
- Log errors server-side (console.error)
- Never expose internal errors to clients
- Handle edge cases (null, undefined, empty arrays)

### 11. Build Configuration
- Ensure `next.config.mjs` exports valid configuration
- Check `tsconfig.json` paths are correct
- Verify `package.json` scripts are correct
- No circular dependencies
- All imports must resolve

### 12. Runtime Configuration
- Use `export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic'` only when necessary
- Use `export const revalidate` for ISR when appropriate
- Default to static generation when possible for better performance

### 13. Cookie Handling
- Use secure cookies in production: `secure: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production'`
- Set proper cookie options (path, maxAge, sameSite)
- Use Edge-compatible cookie signing in middleware

### 14. Absolute URLs
- Use `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL` or `process.env.VERCEL_URL` for absolute URLs
- Never hardcode localhost URLs
- Check for URL existence before using

Example:
```typescript
const baseUrl = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL || 
  (process.env.VERCEL_URL ? `https://${process.env.VERCEL_URL}` : 'http://localhost:3000');
```

### 15. Database Queries
- Always handle Supabase query errors
- Check for PGRST116 (not found) vs other errors
- Use proper TypeScript types from Database types
- Never expose raw database errors to clients

### 16. Pre-Deployment Checklist
Before pushing to Vercel, ensure:
- [ ] `npm run build` succeeds locally
- [ ] All TypeScript errors are resolved
- [ ] All environment variables are set in Vercel dashboard
- [ ] No console.log statements with sensitive data
- [ ] Error handling is in place for all API routes
- [ ] No hardcoded secrets or API keys
- [ ] Middleware doesn't use Node.js APIs
- [ ] All imports resolve correctly
- [ ] Server/Client component boundaries are correct

### 17. Common Pitfalls to Avoid
- ❌ Using `fs` in API routes (use Supabase Storage)
- ❌ Using Node.js APIs in middleware
- ❌ Missing error handling in API routes
- ❌ Exposing service role keys
- ❌ Hardcoded URLs or localhost references
- ❌ Missing environment variable checks
- ❌ Using 'use client' in server-only code
- ❌ Missing tenant context in multi-tenant operations
- ❌ TypeScript errors in production build
- ❌ Circular dependencies

### 18. When Making Changes
1. Check if the change affects server/client boundaries
2. Verify environment variables are available
3. Test error cases (missing data, network failures)
4. Ensure TypeScript types are correct
5. Run build locally before pushing
6. Check Vercel build logs for warnings

## Code Review Checklist
When reviewing code for Vercel deployment:
- ✅ All env vars are checked before use
- ✅ Error handling is comprehensive
- ✅ No Node.js APIs in Edge runtime
- ✅ Proper server/client component usage
- ✅ TypeScript builds without errors
- ✅ Multi-tenant context is handled
- ✅ No hardcoded secrets or URLs
- ✅ Supabase clients are used correctly

